# RNU4-2 BID-seq Nanopore Pipeline

A complete, production-ready pipeline for mapping **pseudouridine (Ψ) sites** in human U4 snRNA (RNU4-2) from nanopore sequencing data using the BID-seq chemical labelling strategy, with integrated detection of the **RENU syndrome n.64_65insT variant**.

---

## Background

### What is BID-seq?

BID-seq (Bisulfite-Induced Deletion sequencing) exploits the selective reactivity of bisulfite with pseudouridine (Ψ):

1. Bisulfite selectively reacts with Ψ (but not unmodified U) to form a stable Ψ-bisulfite adduct
2. When reverse transcriptase encounters this adduct it stalls and **skips the modified nucleotide**, producing a deletion in the cDNA
3. Deep sequencing of this cDNA reveals a characteristic **high deletion rate at Ψ positions** relative to the nanopore background

```
Reference:  ...A  [Ψ]  G  C...
                  ↓ bisulfite
cDNA:       ...A  ---  G  C...     ← deletion at the Ψ site
```

True BID-seq signal: **30–70% deletion rate** at Ψ positions  
Nanopore background: **5–8% deletion rate** (R9.4.1) or **2–4%** (R10.4.1)

### Why is RNU4-2 special?

**RNU4-2** encodes the 141-nt human U4C snRNA, a component of the U4/U6•U5 tri-snRNP complex of the major spliceosome. U4 snRNA is critical for:
- U4/U6 snRNA base-pairing during spliceosome assembly
- Positioning of the U6 ACAGAGA loop at 5' splice sites
- Catalytic activation of splicing

**RENU syndrome** is caused by de novo variants in RNU4-2, most commonly the **n.64_65insT** insertion — a single T inserted between positions 64 and 65 within the T-loop quasi-pseudoknot. This disrupts U4/U6 snRNA base-pairing and impairs splicing globally.

---

## Pipeline Overview

```
Input FASTQ (BID-seq treated)
  │
  ├─► NanoStat QC → Read filtering (nanoq)
  │
  ├─► minimap2 alignment → sorted BAM
  │
  ├─► Position-resolved pileup (pysam)
  │     ├── deletion_count, deletion_rate per position
  │     └── insertion events (for RENU tracking)
  │
  ├─► Statistical analysis
  │     ├── Paired mode:  Fisher's exact test (treated vs control)
  │     └── Single mode:  Binomial test vs estimated background
  │         └── Benjamini-Hochberg FDR correction
  │
  ├─► Pseudouridine caller
  │     ├── HIGH_CONFIDENCE | MODERATE | LOW | NOISE tiers
  │     ├── Known Ψ annotation (positions 4, 72, 79)
  │     └── BED6 export for IGV
  │
  ├─► RENU tracker (n.64_65insT)
  │     ├── T insertion detection at position 64
  │     ├── VAF calculation
  │     └── Zygosity inference (WT / HET / HOM / MOSAIC)
  │
  └─► HTML Report
        ├── Coverage plot
        ├── Deletion rate landscape
        ├── Volcano plot
        ├── Known Ψ recovery bar chart
        ├── RENU insertion pie chart
        └── Full per-position statistics table
```

---

## Installation

### Requirements

- Linux or macOS (WSL2 on Windows)
- [Miniconda](https://docs.conda.io/en/latest/miniconda.html) or Anaconda
- Python ≥ 3.10

### One-command install

```bash
git clone <this-repo>
cd rnu4_bidseq_pipeline
bash scripts/install.sh
conda activate bidseq_nanopore
```

This will:
1. Create the `bidseq_nanopore` conda environment
2. Install all Python and bioinformatics dependencies (minimap2, samtools, pysam, scipy, etc.)
3. Install the `bidseq` command-line tool
4. Fetch the RNU4-2 reference from NCBI (NR_002715)
5. Run the test suite to verify everything works

### Manual installation

```bash
conda env create -f environment.yml
conda activate bidseq_nanopore
pip install -e .
bidseq fetch-reference --output reference/rnu4_2_reference.fa
```

---

## Usage

### Quick start — single sample

```bash
bidseq run \
  --treated  my_bidseq_sample.fastq.gz \
  --output   results/my_sample/
```

### Paired run (strongly recommended)

```bash
bidseq run \
  --treated  treated.fastq.gz \
  --control  untreated_ctrl.fastq.gz \
  --output   results/paired_analysis/
```

### With a custom reference

```bash
bidseq run \
  --treated    sample.fastq.gz \
  --reference  reference/my_rnu4_2.fa \
  --output     results/
```

### Call from a pre-aligned BAM

```bash
bidseq call \
  --treated  sample.bam \
  --control  control.bam \
  --output   results/
```

### All options

```
bidseq run --help

Options:
  -t, --treated TEXT          BID-seq treated FASTQ (or FASTQ.gz)  [required]
  -c, --control TEXT          Untreated control FASTQ (optional)
  -r, --reference TEXT        Reference FASTA (fetched from NCBI if omitted)
  -o, --output TEXT           Output directory  [required]
  -s, --sample TEXT           Sample name for report
  -m, --mode [cdna|direct_rna]  Sequencing mode (default: cdna)
  -p, --threads INTEGER       CPU threads (default: 4)
  --config TEXT               YAML config override
  --min-coverage INTEGER      Minimum depth per position (default: 20)
  --min-del-rate FLOAT        Minimum deletion rate to call (default: 0.12)
  --fdr FLOAT                 FDR threshold (default: 0.05)
  --background FLOAT          Nanopore background rate prior (default: 0.06)
  -v, --verbose               Verbose logging
```

---

## Output Files

After a successful run, the output directory contains:

| File | Description |
|------|-------------|
| `{sample}_report.html` | **Main report** — self-contained HTML with all plots and tables |
| `{sample}_pseudoU_calls.tsv` | Called pseudouridine sites with statistics |
| `{sample}_pseudoU_calls.bed` | BED6 file for IGV/genome browser |
| `{sample}_deletion_stats.tsv` | Per-position deletion statistics (all positions) |
| `{sample}_pileup.tsv` | Raw pileup counts |
| `{sample}_renu_summary.json` | RENU n.64_65insT insertion analysis |
| `{sample}_summary.json` | Run-level QC and call summary |
| `pipeline.log` | Full log file |
| `treated/{sample}.bam` | Sorted, indexed BAM |

---

## Statistical Methods

### Why this is hard for nanopore

Nanopore cDNA sequencing has a **5–8% background deletion rate** at any position (arising from polymerase slippage and basecalling errors). BID-seq induces **30–70% deletion rates** at true Ψ sites. The signal-to-noise ratio is approximately 8:1, requiring careful statistics.

### Background rate estimation

In single-sample mode, the background deletion rate is estimated from the treated sample itself:

1. Calculate deletion rate at every position
2. Take positions in the lowest 25th percentile
3. Use their median as the background rate
4. Apply a nanopore-specific floor (default 6%) to prevent underestimation

### Statistical tests

**Paired mode (treated + control):**
- Fisher's exact test (one-sided: treated deletion rate > control)
- 2×2 contingency table per position: `[del_t, cov_t-del_t] vs [del_c, cov_c-del_c]`

**Single-sample mode:**
- One-sided binomial test: `P(X ≥ del_count | n=coverage, p=background_rate)`

All p-values are corrected for multiple testing using Benjamini-Hochberg FDR.

### Confidence tiers

| Tier | q-value | Fold change | Deletion rate |
|------|---------|-------------|---------------|
| HIGH_CONFIDENCE | < 0.01 | ≥ 5× | ≥ 25% |
| MODERATE | < 0.05 | ≥ 3× | ≥ 15% |
| LOW | < 0.10 | ≥ 2× | ≥ 12% |
| NOISE | — | — | — |

### Hard exclusions

Sites are always classified as NOISE if:
- Coverage < 20 reads
- Reference base is not T/U (BID-seq only acts on Ψ, which looks like U)
- Deletion rate < `min_deletion_rate` threshold

### Homopolymer flagging

Positions within homopolymer runs (≥4 identical bases) are flagged in the output. Nanopore has elevated indel error rates in these contexts, so calls here should be interpreted with caution.

---

## RENU Syndrome Variant Detection

The pipeline specifically tracks the **n.64_65insT** RENU insertion:

1. Scans pileup at reference position 64 for insertions after that base
2. Classifies each read as: WILD_TYPE / RENU_T_INS / OTHER_INS
3. Calculates VAF = RENU reads / total reads
4. Sequence-context check: flanking bases must match reference (max 1 mismatch) to filter nanopore artefacts

**Zygosity inference:**

| VAF | Interpretation |
|-----|----------------|
| < 2% or < 3 reads | WILD_TYPE |
| 2–30% | MOSAIC_OR_LOW_SIGNAL |
| 30–70% | HETEROZYGOUS |
| 70–85% | NEAR_HOMOZYGOUS |
| > 85% | HOMOZYGOUS |

Note: The recurrent RENU mutation is typically de novo heterozygous. A VAF of 40–60% is expected for germline heterozygous cases. Nanopore VAF estimates can have ±5–10% variance.

---

## Configuration

Override any parameter via a YAML config file:

```bash
bidseq run --treated sample.fastq --config my_config.yaml --output results/
```

Example config:

```yaml
statistics:
  min_deletion_rate: 0.15      # stricter threshold
  min_fold_change: 4.0
  fdr_threshold: 0.01
  nanopore_background_deletion_rate: 0.04  # if using R10.4.1 chemistry

qc:
  min_coverage: 50             # require higher coverage

reference:
  known_pseudoU_sites: [4, 72, 79]   # update if new sites discovered
```

Full parameter reference: see `config/default_config.yaml`.

